WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:08.059
Very excited to have a distinguished professor
and Presidential Chair in Information Studies

00:00:08.059 --> 00:00:10.190
at UCLA, Christine Borgman.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:16.039
She is highly accomplished and she's the author
of more than 250 publications in information

00:00:16.039 --> 00:00:19.070
studies, computer science, and communication.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:23.609
And at UCLA she directs the Center for Knowledge
Infrastructures with research grants from

00:00:23.609 --> 00:00:28.269
the Alfred P Sloan Foundation, the National
Science Foundation, and many other sources.

00:00:28.269 --> 00:00:35.640
She is a world authority on data science,
data sharing, information sciences and how

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.489
that can be communicated effectively.

00:00:37.489 --> 00:00:43.120
She is the author of several books, including
one I'm sure she's going to mention in her

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.120
presentation.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.850
We're absolutely delighted to have her.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:51.040
As well as her colleague Irene Pasquetto,
who's a research assistant also at the Center

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.090
for Knowledge Infrastructures.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:58.370
She's also a PhD candidate in the Department
of Information Studies at UCLA.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:03.199
And she's working on her dissertation on data
sharing, reuse practices in molecular biology

00:01:03.199 --> 00:01:04.530
and genomics.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:09.490
And she has research interests in open science
frameworks, science governance models, and

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:16.280
in more general, the ethics and policies behind
data sharing, code sharing, and other practices

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:17.280
for open science.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.759
We're absolutely delighted to have the both
of you.

00:01:19.759 --> 00:01:24.759
We look forward to your presentation I'd like
to take this opportunity to remind our listeners

00:01:24.759 --> 00:01:29.619
that you have an opportunity to send in your
questions via the little GoToWebinar question

00:01:29.619 --> 00:01:32.369
submission system during the talk.

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:38.049
In the last few minutes of the hour, we'll
take some time to read off some of those questions

00:01:38.049 --> 00:01:40.270
for our speakers and get some answers to them.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:46.320
So without further delay, Dr. Borgman thank
you so much for taking the time to speak with

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:47.570
us today.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:50.390
And Dr. Pasquetto as well.

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:54.270
Thank you, Jack and [? Crystal ?] and to the
whole group.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:57.579
And it's an honor to be part of this.

00:01:57.579 --> 00:02:03.820
As you said in the introductions, we've been
working with the National Science Foundation

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.830
and the Sloan Foundation.

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:11.150
And a little bit more indirectly with the
National Institutes of Health.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:16.400
Because one of the projects that we're studying
that Irene will talk about, as the case study

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.810
here that is her dissertation, is funded by
NIH.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:26.250
And then I'm of course, a number of coordinating
bodies, including a strategic planning panel

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:32.080
for data for the NIH and National Library
of Medicine.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:38.530
Why data sharing and reuse are hard to do
is a huge and book-length topic.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:42.080
Certainly more than we can do in 45 minutes
or so today.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:47.640
So what we hope to do is give you a larger
frame, and point you to some other sources

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.200
to think about it.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.900
I'll spend the first 15 or 20 minutes giving
a big picture, and then Irene will do her

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.150
case study.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.870
And I'll come back and do the wrap-up.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:07.070
For starters I want to give you this larger
framing around data sharing policies.

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:13.380
We've been thinking about open access to publications
and open data in various ways for several

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:14.380
decades.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:21.090
And we've had open data repositories
for 50 years or more in areas such as genomics,

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.920
social sciences, surveys, and so on.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.760
But it's really been ramping up in the last
5-10 years.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:34.650
The [? Holdren ?] memo from the White House
and the Obama Administration was the one that

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:41.180
required open access to publications and to
data for all US large funding agencies.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:50.680
The European Commission has been even more
aggressive in declaring that open science--

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:56.400
open-access publications-- and open data are
official policy of the European Union.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.600
And so all new grants going forward are required
to have open data.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:08.650
They are making very specific plans, not only
through the rest of the [? horizon ?] [? 2020,

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:14.200
?] but into the 9th framework program, which
will run from 2021 to 2028.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:19.810
And really thinking about this as the future
approach.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:27.670
So the challenge then is to think about how
you actually accomplish open data and reuse

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:30.380
on the ground.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:37.580
Now the book of mine that Dr. Van Horn mentioned,
that came out about two years ago from MIT

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:43.340
Press, I surveyed a wide range of policies
up to that point.

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:50.800
And I was able to categorize the various motivations
and arguments in these public policy documents

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:53.710
into four general categories.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:05:00.830
The one that you hear most often is that research
data should be shared, so that we can reproduce

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:01.830
research.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:05.120
And in some cases that's feasible and possible.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:10.310
But it's actually extremely difficult and
extremely contentious in most fields, as to

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:13.860
what reproducibility actually means.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:18.910
It tends to be in more computational areas
that reproducibility is actually feasible

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:20.760
or accomplishable.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:21.760
We can do things.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:27.130
We can verify, we can validate, we can replicate,
but true reproducibility is probably the biggest

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:28.990
challenge of all.

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:34.620
The second category is making public assets
available to the public.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:42.080
On the grounds that most big science, including
most public health and medicine research,

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:49.800
is accomplished with public funding-- with
taxpayer dollars, euros, yen and so on-- is

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:55.750
that the public should have access to the
publications and to the data.

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:58.370
They should be useful for research purposes.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:02.340
Thirdly to leverage the investments.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:08.139
It costs a great deal of money, no matter
what the currency [? is, ?] to accomplish

00:06:08.139 --> 00:06:10.500
the research and to produce the data.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:15.889
So in what ways can we leverage those data
and make them useful for other purposes across

00:06:15.889 --> 00:06:16.889
time?

00:06:16.889 --> 00:06:17.930
Again, not easy to do.

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:20.490
But these are large goals.

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:28.620
The last one, which in many respects is cumulative,
is to advance research and innovation.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:34.640
On the-- at least theoretical-- grounds that
if you make data more widely available, other

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:37.350
people can use it for [? unseen ?] purposes.

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:43.340
Ask new questions, build new programs, even
build new companies.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:49.389
So all of those reasons are why public policy
around the world, especially in the western

00:06:49.389 --> 00:06:57.030
world, are moving very quickly in the direction
of open science and open data.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:07:03.360
However we also know from a decade or two
of social science research that it's hard

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:09.830
to do for many reasons, not
the least of which is the lack of incentives.

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:13.470
Scholars collaborate with each other but they
also compete.

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:18.860
The first one to a finding gets priority,
no matter what the prize is, whatever the

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:22.980
journal article is, whatever the best job
is.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:28.500
Scholars in all fields, including biomedicine,
are rewarded much more for publications than

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:30.770
for releasing data.

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:37.060
One of the aspects of these problems that
our group is looking at in most depth is around

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:42.310
the practices associated with documenting
data, and making them available in a form

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:43.980
that other people can use.

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:45.840
And we'll talk about that today.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:53.419
The skills to document metadata [? provenance
?] version control, connecting data to the

00:07:53.419 --> 00:07:59.340
software, the tools, the calibration, the
instruments, is often a set of skills that's

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:05.500
different from the set of skills that's required
to accomplish the research.

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:12.690
The information studies field is a part of
the data sciences where we're trying to educate

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:17.949
a new generation of professionals to facilitate
those aspects.

00:08:17.949 --> 00:08:24.220
Lastly but by no means least are questions
of control and of ownership.

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:28.570
It's contentious as to who actually owns data.

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:32.070
You often have many hands many, many universities
in the pot.

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:35.240
You've got different kinds of funding models.

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:43.240
This is a wide open area, and questions of
legal interoperability are among those on

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:48.880
the table in larger discussions of open access
to data.

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:55.860
Where our group is spending its time is, right
now I'm really looking at questions of reuse.

00:08:55.860 --> 00:09:01.550
In our opinion data sharing should not be
viewed as an end in itself.

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:06.930
Releasing the data doesn't get you very far
if you don't release them in such a way that

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:09.600
other people actually can reuse them.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:15.500
And these are some of the reasons that you
might want to reuse them to reproduce-- as

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:21.320
we talked about-- to replicate, to do it again--
not necessarily by the same methods and means--

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:25.200
to verify, to validate.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:31.570
We have found in our other research in other
fields over the years that sometimes people

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:35.440
are seeking data to reuse to ask new questions.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:39.640
But other times they're using them for validation
or comparison.

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:42.100
Almost background purposes.

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:48.380
Getting them from, say, government sources
of field ground-- ground truthing, it's called

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:51.660
in the environmental sciences.

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:56.310
And lastly to integrate with other data, and
you'll be hearing more about that in the case

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:57.890
study.

00:09:57.890 --> 00:10:04.520
Integrating requires cleaning, reconciling,
standardizing in ways that also can be a major

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:09.260
undertaking in and of themselves.

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:12.500
So now to think about what are data in the
first place.

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:18.230
We've also found that one person's signal
is someone else's noise.

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:24.020
Just deciding what are the data out of a research
project in the first place can be one of the

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:31.180
most daunting challenges to get to the place
where you could actually make data usable

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:35.580
by some other parties.

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:42.420
Here's an example that is on the one hand,
a bit far afield from biomedicine.

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:48.380
But one that's straight forward to explain
the signal-to-noise issues.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:53.760
We were part of the Center for Embedded Network
Sensing for the 10 years of its existence

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:58.620
as a National Science Foundation science and
technology center.

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:04.380
This image is one from networked infomechanical
systems, built by

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:08.240
some electrical engineering teams and many
partners.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:15.890
And it was a huge advance forward in environmental
sciences, because you could get data at far

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:23.650
more granularity and more speed from the air,
the water, the ground and look for things

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:31.840
like different kinds of harmful algal blooms,
looking at things like methylmercury contamination

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:33.110
in rice fields.

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:39.210
Some very important environmental problems
being studied this way.

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:44.630
As we would interview people, we would find
things like an engineering researcher would

00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:47.070
say, temperature's temperature.

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:53.209
If he's getting the same signal from one day
to the next on his temperature sensor, he's

00:11:53.209 --> 00:11:54.890
happy, he thinks it's working.

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:59.660
And for his standards, for his kinds of science,
it's adequate.

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:04.770
We asked a biologist on the same team the
same question.

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:09.780
And this long narrative here on the right
is just a sample from what we got.

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:15.870
He has to deal with international standards
set by his field about the way in which you

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:18.280
trust temperature.

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:23.620
And the measurements that the engineer would
get was not adequate.

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:28.630
He could not publish in a biological journal
with those measurements, because they view

00:12:28.630 --> 00:12:31.480
them very, very differently.

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:34.860
Now neither of these people is right or wrong.

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:38.529
They're both correct with respect to their
field.

00:12:38.529 --> 00:12:44.040
And that, I think, again is one of the fundamental
challenges with data sharing.

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:51.370
This image here and the quote from my book,
the Big Data, Little Data, No Data, is where

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:58.640
I ended up with a somewhat phenomenological
definition of data.

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:05.190
To say that they are representations that
become data at the point that you use them

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:07.430
as evidence of phenomena.

00:13:07.430 --> 00:13:13.180
So almost anything in our world could be data,
but it's not data and you declare it to be

00:13:13.180 --> 00:13:15.510
data and declare it to be some kind of evidence.

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:21.370
Again, much of what we do is look at those
processes by which someone's signals become

00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:26.450
evidence or some kind of phenomena.

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:33.570
This next slide gives you the title of our
current large three-year grant from the Sloan

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:37.880
Foundation, which is if data sharing is the
answer, what is the question?

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:44.529
So we're looking broadly at why people are
trying to share data, and what it is they're

00:13:44.529 --> 00:13:46.030
trying to accomplish.

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:50.810
So we're trying to explicate these concepts
such as data sharing, reuse, openness, and

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:56.320
infrastructure across different kinds of domains,
look at how practice is changing.

00:13:56.320 --> 00:14:01.459
And we've called out these three factors,
which again I'll discuss briefly.

00:14:01.459 --> 00:14:08.830
Those mixtures or combinations or configurations
of expertise from different disciplines, scale

00:14:08.830 --> 00:14:09.830
factors.

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:16.640
Is it a matter of the volume of data, the
time period, or what other factors?

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:22.649
Then thirdly-- and this is the centralization
one-- really comes to bear in questions of

00:14:22.649 --> 00:14:24.170
biomedical research.

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:30.060
So again I'll go through very briefly, and
note that we have publications on all of these

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:37.250
topics already that you'll find linked from
the Center for Knowledge Infrastructures website.

00:14:37.250 --> 00:14:39.170
We use qualitative methods.

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:42.940
We do a lot of digging of publications and
web sites.

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:49.070
We try to get the official story and the unofficial
story about what people are trying to accomplish.

00:14:49.070 --> 00:14:54.870
Our grad students and researchers spend
long periods of time on-site.

00:14:54.870 --> 00:15:00.420
Irene has been working with this particular
biomedical group for about 2 and 1/2 years

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:08.140
already, really working with them, visiting
their sites around other parts of the US.

00:15:08.140 --> 00:15:14.600
And only when we understand a site well enough
to be able to ask really specific questions

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:16.990
do we come in and start doing interviews.

00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:21.400
And she'll talk a bit more about how many
interviews and where she is in the process

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:25.410
for this particular study.

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:29.730
Here's a sampling of the sites that we're
in right now.

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:36.320
We're not that big a team as it looks like
this, but we have been in some of these for

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:42.200
nearly 10 years, and some of them for only
a year or so.

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:44.340
And I won't talk about them in any depth.

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:49.850
The order in here is more about how long we've
been looking at them.

00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:55.279
What's useful it is that we have enough breadth
to get some strong comparisons.

00:15:55.279 --> 00:16:01.020
Looking at astronomy that's focused on a place,
the sky and the universe.

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:04.519
The earth sciences, where they're drilling
under the sea floor.

00:16:04.519 --> 00:16:11.770
The biomedical and the computational science
are both looking at particular kinds of problems.

00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:18.230
And the biomedical one is around data sharing
and reuse in what is remarkably interdisciplinary

00:16:18.230 --> 00:16:23.810
context compared to the kinds of research
usually done in these fields.

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:29.910
The first question, then, is that one of mixtures.

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:35.430
No matter how narrow a field looks, when you
start digging deeper you find that people

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:41.920
have graduate degrees from different disciplines,
different departments, different theoretical

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:42.920
orientations.

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:49.899
They use different software, different instrumentation/
all of these things come together to influence

00:16:49.899 --> 00:16:54.240
the collection, the use, and the reuse of
data.

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:59.089
The Sloan Digital Sky Survey which has been
running-- actually the main parts of it, the

00:16:59.089 --> 00:17:02.860
first two parts ran from 2000 to 2008.

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:06.529
But it was a decade or two before that for
the design.

00:17:06.529 --> 00:17:14.669
The 160 or more terabytes of data looked like
a vast amount at the time they were building

00:17:14.669 --> 00:17:15.669
it.

00:17:15.669 --> 00:17:17.230
It's not so big by today's standards.

00:17:17.230 --> 00:17:22.720
Again, the tens of millions of dollars that
they spent, the new projects are much bigger

00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:23.959
than that.

00:17:23.959 --> 00:17:29.940
The Sloan Digital Sky Survey is notable because
it was built to be open data to the world

00:17:29.940 --> 00:17:33.179
for education and research from the first
day.

00:17:33.179 --> 00:17:40.190
But it was built on proprietary software in
collaboration with Jim Gray of the Microsoft

00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:46.200
Corporation, who's also a Turing Award winner
in computer science.

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:53.070
The Large Synoptic Survey Telescope, that
is going to get 15 terabytes per night.

00:17:53.070 --> 00:17:56.129
And they're spending more than a billion dollars
on it.

00:17:56.129 --> 00:17:58.830
It's well over a decade along already.

00:17:58.830 --> 00:18:01.149
And we're about five years out.

00:18:01.149 --> 00:18:04.710
The target of course keeps moving forward.

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:07.570
Then they plan to get data for about 10 years.

00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:16.299
There, they've got the problem of how you
actually build a data commons, how you deal

00:18:16.299 --> 00:18:21.001
with free rider problems, and they're still
brokering what partners are going to have

00:18:21.001 --> 00:18:22.001
access to it.

00:18:22.001 --> 00:18:24.509
But they're investing in open source software.

00:18:24.509 --> 00:18:28.669
So they're taking a different route even than
Sloan did in how they're approaching open

00:18:28.669 --> 00:18:29.669
data.

00:18:29.669 --> 00:18:32.070
What are the mixtures?

00:18:32.070 --> 00:18:35.520
Many of these astronomers have all of their
degrees in physics.

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:40.080
Others come up through much more observational
astronomy, a great deal of computer sciences

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:41.080
mixed in.

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:46.139
One of the people we're working with takes
PhD students in astronomy, in physics, and

00:18:46.139 --> 00:18:49.870
in computer science.

00:18:49.870 --> 00:18:52.710
This project is a mature discipline.

00:18:52.710 --> 00:18:55.980
They have vast amounts of data, abundant data.

00:18:55.980 --> 00:19:00.149
They have a long history of the infrastructure,
trusted archives.

00:19:00.149 --> 00:19:03.570
And they've been doing these methods for a
long time.

00:19:03.570 --> 00:19:06.169
And that's true across all of these.

00:19:06.169 --> 00:19:14.239
By comparison, in the sciences, we've been
looking at the C-DEBI the Center for Dark

00:19:14.239 --> 00:19:19.279
Energy Biosphere Investigations, that gets
most of its data by drilling under the sea

00:19:19.279 --> 00:19:20.279
floor.

00:19:20.279 --> 00:19:26.320
This also is a National Science Foundation
science and technology center that collaborates

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:31.970
at about 35 institutions across the US, and
then it made grants to those even more.

00:19:31.970 --> 00:19:36.330
This photo on the right is what we might call
a library.

00:19:36.330 --> 00:19:37.509
It's a repository.

00:19:37.509 --> 00:19:39.559
It's an open data repository.

00:19:39.559 --> 00:19:44.409
But those are frozen ice cores from under
the sea floor and those specimens are their

00:19:44.409 --> 00:19:45.970
primary data source.

00:19:45.970 --> 00:19:52.109
This drawing that our collaborator, Peter
Darch, has driven shows you how all those

00:19:52.109 --> 00:19:55.259
cores come off one set of cruises.

00:19:55.259 --> 00:20:02.100
But the physical science people take them
down one path, and the microbiological-- the

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:05.309
ones we're studying-- take them down another
path.

00:20:05.309 --> 00:20:12.739
I think what was most striking to us here
is that decisions that are made on-board the

00:20:12.739 --> 00:20:17.720
ship are absolutely critical to who can make
data out of these.

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:25.279
For the physical sciences, the line down the
left, you can store those cores at minus 4

00:20:25.279 --> 00:20:26.279
centigrade.

00:20:26.279 --> 00:20:31.899
For the microbiological down the right, you
have to be able to store them at minus 80

00:20:31.899 --> 00:20:32.899
centigrade.

00:20:32.899 --> 00:20:37.660
So unless those are frozen cold enough on
the ship, they can never again be used for

00:20:37.660 --> 00:20:40.179
biological research.

00:20:40.179 --> 00:20:46.470
So those early decisions are critical long-term
in use and in reuse of data.

00:20:46.470 --> 00:20:50.729
So here the mixtures are on the domains.

00:20:50.729 --> 00:20:57.039
Many different specialties, and again we have
papers on this about the mix of them working

00:20:57.039 --> 00:20:58.039
together.

00:20:58.039 --> 00:21:00.289
This is a much more emergent problem.

00:21:00.289 --> 00:21:02.330
The data are scarce.

00:21:02.330 --> 00:21:08.549
They are not able to set standards yet because
you get one core here, you get one core there.

00:21:08.549 --> 00:21:11.629
Many different questions and very exploratory
kinds of methods.

00:21:11.629 --> 00:21:16.649
So they're trying to build capacity as opposed
to the huge capacity that exists in astronomy.

00:21:16.649 --> 00:21:21.190
I'm going to go brief on these other couple
of questions so that we have time for the

00:21:21.190 --> 00:21:23.039
case study.

00:21:23.039 --> 00:21:25.440
The scale factors.

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:28.669
Big data is usually what people think is the
problem.

00:21:28.669 --> 00:21:32.870
But rather, we're seeing many other kinds
of scale are critical.

00:21:32.870 --> 00:21:37.399
This notion of the three V's goes back a decade
or so.

00:21:37.399 --> 00:21:43.840
Goes from the business world to say that the
complexity of data is a function not only

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:50.049
in volume, but of the variety, the heterogeneity,
and the velocity, the speed at which it's

00:21:50.049 --> 00:21:51.779
coming at you.

00:21:51.779 --> 00:21:55.539
We see the temporal characteristics are important.

00:21:55.539 --> 00:22:02.110
The spatial characteristics and the variety
of personnel and the number of personnel also

00:22:02.110 --> 00:22:04.879
to be key.

00:22:04.879 --> 00:22:11.879
Here's just one example of that, is to look
at the two astronomy projects.

00:22:11.879 --> 00:22:13.330
Look at this range of time.

00:22:13.330 --> 00:22:20.659
45 years from the time of inception of the
first one of these, until the end of the data

00:22:20.659 --> 00:22:22.389
collection and beyond.

00:22:22.389 --> 00:22:28.320
The kinds of uncertainty these people have
to deal with is also how they're thinking

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:33.470
about how you build an open source, how you
think about data, how you think about sharing.

00:22:33.470 --> 00:22:38.899
So again briefly on the scale factors, and
we've published some of these elsewhere for

00:22:38.899 --> 00:22:41.639
you to think about.

00:22:41.639 --> 00:22:44.499
But there's the uncertainty in the long temporal.

00:22:44.499 --> 00:22:47.649
There's the scarce data that are sparse.

00:22:47.649 --> 00:22:49.759
There's the variety of genomes studied.

00:22:49.759 --> 00:22:55.320
And then, again, big differences in some of
these computational areas we're looking at.

00:22:55.320 --> 00:23:00.299
Thirdly-- and I do want to use this as a setup
for a [? remnants ?] case study around how

00:23:00.299 --> 00:23:03.419
the degree of centralization matters.

00:23:03.419 --> 00:23:13.240
We tried to compare what happens in areas
like astronomy, where you spent a decade building

00:23:13.240 --> 00:23:19.090
the instruments, building the data management
functions, so that you can pull a large archive

00:23:19.090 --> 00:23:21.049
together that everybody can use.

00:23:21.049 --> 00:23:26.919
As opposed to working independently, which
is much more common in biomedical.

00:23:26.919 --> 00:23:29.159
And trying to integrate them later.

00:23:29.159 --> 00:23:36.869
That, in many respects, is a much harder problem
than the kind of problem that astronomy is

00:23:36.869 --> 00:23:39.289
dealing with.

00:23:39.289 --> 00:23:43.629
So with that, let me turn it over to Irene
who's sitting here next to me.

00:23:43.629 --> 00:23:46.590
And she will give you the example.

00:23:46.590 --> 00:23:51.639
And again she's been in here for almost 2
and 1/2 years now.

00:23:51.639 --> 00:23:56.809
The dissertation will come out about a year
from now in spring of 2018.

00:23:56.809 --> 00:24:02.919
And I think that it's going to be a very important
contribution, not only to information studies,

00:24:02.919 --> 00:24:06.720
but to the biomedical areas in thinking about
reuse.

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:07.720
So, Irene.

00:24:07.720 --> 00:24:10.090
Good morning, this is Irene.

00:24:10.090 --> 00:24:15.809
Thank you Chris for the great overview on
this important topic.

00:24:15.809 --> 00:24:20.649
In my dissertation work I really look at the
practices of better use and integration in

00:24:20.649 --> 00:24:22.330
the scientific collaboration.

00:24:22.330 --> 00:24:29.630
I'm very interested in looking at what happens
to research data once they are made available,

00:24:29.630 --> 00:24:31.279
once they are shared.

00:24:31.279 --> 00:24:37.840
So I'm asking questions such as, who is reusing
the data once they're available in open repositories.

00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:39.690
Why and how?

00:24:39.690 --> 00:24:46.259
My main case study is consortium for data
sharing biomedicine.

00:24:46.259 --> 00:24:54.769
Over the last 2 years and 1/2, I conducted
over 100 hours of interviews and observations.

00:24:54.769 --> 00:25:00.509
And over 30 interviews with the participants
of this consortium.

00:25:00.509 --> 00:25:04.049
We have 10 labs involved in this collaboration.

00:25:04.049 --> 00:25:11.429
And they are geographically distributed all
over the United States, plus a team of engineers.

00:25:11.429 --> 00:25:14.869
The scientists investigated the [? body.

00:25:14.869 --> 00:25:19.269
?] In this collaboration, they investigated
the developmental and genetic factors involved

00:25:19.269 --> 00:25:21.480
in a set of rare syndromes.

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:26.600
It is, as you can understand, a highly interdisciplinary
effort.

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:31.340
Indeed, the investigators span the molecular
and developmental biology.

00:25:31.340 --> 00:25:34.190
Computational biology, [? Maggie's ?] [? team,
?]

00:25:34.190 --> 00:25:36.989
computer engineers but also computer modeling.

00:25:36.989 --> 00:25:44.889
The goal behind this collaboration is really
to fund a collection of these new and advanced

00:25:44.889 --> 00:25:49.389
genomic and imaging data that is related to
these rare syndromes.

00:25:49.389 --> 00:25:57.899
And by making all the data openly available
for use, the goal is to encourage collaborative

00:25:57.899 --> 00:26:01.379
efforts and systemic approaches to [? knowledge
?] [? and ?] discovery.

00:26:01.379 --> 00:26:08.289
Also to make the data sharing process and
reuse [? fostering ?] also collaboration happening

00:26:08.289 --> 00:26:10.489
faster.

00:26:10.489 --> 00:26:17.559
Scientists who are collaborating in this consortium,
they are expected to make all their research

00:26:17.559 --> 00:26:21.450
data available prior to publication.

00:26:21.450 --> 00:26:28.820
Which means that as soon as the data are collected,
cleaned, curated, they are ready to be shipped.

00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:33.169
Just a little bit more about this consortium
and their work.

00:26:33.169 --> 00:26:40.320
The data collected this this community includes
patients images, the metric [? score ?] anatomy

00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:41.320
norms.

00:26:41.320 --> 00:26:45.729
Phenotypic images and sequences from [? genomic-wide
?] association studies.

00:26:45.729 --> 00:26:48.529
But there's the results from gene proteins
functional validation studies.

00:26:48.529 --> 00:26:56.559
This is one of the challenges of these consortiums,
that data are collected from four different

00:26:56.559 --> 00:26:59.390
model organisms.

00:26:59.390 --> 00:27:03.970
So we have humans, mice, zebra fish, and chimpanzee.

00:27:03.970 --> 00:27:14.470
So as you understand, it's a really heterogeneous
set of actors.

00:27:14.470 --> 00:27:16.592
So I want to present now my findings so far.

00:27:16.592 --> 00:27:20.590
As Chris was saying I will graduate in a year
or so.

00:27:20.590 --> 00:27:23.629
So I'm still in the process of finishing my
data collection.

00:27:23.629 --> 00:27:27.929
But I have quote some data.

00:27:27.929 --> 00:27:34.649
So in terms of data sharing practices, what
we are finding is that most of the labs involve

00:27:34.649 --> 00:27:35.649
them.

00:27:35.649 --> 00:27:42.389
They really didn't have any particular problems
with making their data openly available on

00:27:42.389 --> 00:27:47.450
the shared repository.

00:27:47.450 --> 00:27:50.519
We were partially surprised.

00:27:50.519 --> 00:27:55.029
Because initially we expected a scientist
to be kind of reluctant to share their data

00:27:55.029 --> 00:27:56.590
prior to publication.

00:27:56.590 --> 00:27:58.249
But this really didn't happen.

00:27:58.249 --> 00:28:04.539
Almost all the data sets are indeed collected
by the community and are available on the

00:28:04.539 --> 00:28:08.799
centralized database created by the consortium.

00:28:08.799 --> 00:28:14.809
However, when we look at we should be using
the data and how, a set of concerns and challenges

00:28:14.809 --> 00:28:15.999
emerged.

00:28:15.999 --> 00:28:22.269
So basically what we are finding is that reusing
this unpublished data is much more difficult

00:28:22.269 --> 00:28:25.690
than sharing the data sets.

00:28:25.690 --> 00:28:29.309
We found that data use practices are not homogeneous.

00:28:29.309 --> 00:28:36.190
But they really vary by skills, expertise,
disciplinary focus, and types of data.

00:28:36.190 --> 00:28:43.059
So on one hand, we found some shared concerns
across most of the scientists about data use.

00:28:43.059 --> 00:28:47.309
On the other hand, we found that some disciplinary-specific
challenges.

00:28:47.309 --> 00:28:50.080
So now I'm going to go through my findings.

00:28:50.080 --> 00:28:51.080
OK.

00:28:51.080 --> 00:28:59.379
So let's start with the common issues with
data reuse.

00:28:59.379 --> 00:29:08.450
First, we found that almost all the scientists
involved in this collaboration, they expressed

00:29:08.450 --> 00:29:11.789
concerns over using unpublished data.

00:29:11.789 --> 00:29:17.229
So from the [? funder's ?] point of view,
sharing data prior to publication is of course

00:29:17.229 --> 00:29:23.450
presented as a strategy to increase efficiency
and making collaboration across labs faster.

00:29:23.450 --> 00:29:30.700
However, from a scientist's point of view,
reusing data prior to publication represented

00:29:30.700 --> 00:29:35.119
a breach in the ways of establishing trust
in others' data.

00:29:35.119 --> 00:29:41.190
So most of the scientists reported that when
they have to decide if they want to trust

00:29:41.190 --> 00:29:46.210
or not specifically a given data set, the
fact that that data set is associated to a

00:29:46.210 --> 00:29:52.169
specific study that's being published in academic
journal, plays a really important role for

00:29:52.169 --> 00:29:58.539
them to decide whether or not to trust this
data set.

00:29:58.539 --> 00:29:59.919
Second point.

00:29:59.919 --> 00:30:06.190
We also found that most study participants
expressed concerns over the quality of others'

00:30:06.190 --> 00:30:07.190
data.

00:30:07.190 --> 00:30:13.719
So scientists reported that about multiple
occasions, in which they invested a lot of

00:30:13.719 --> 00:30:21.239
time and effort and resources trying to validate
others' data sets in order to use them.

00:30:21.239 --> 00:30:26.469
However, on multiple occasions they found
these data sets not to be acute, accurate

00:30:26.469 --> 00:30:27.929
or not working.

00:30:27.929 --> 00:30:34.009
For this reason, the scientists-- they tend
to reuse published data mostly in the context

00:30:34.009 --> 00:30:37.981
of a research collaboration where they have
personal knowledge of those who collected

00:30:37.981 --> 00:30:38.981
the data.

00:30:38.981 --> 00:30:45.600
Mainly because they know it is difficult to
go through this process of violating others'

00:30:45.600 --> 00:30:47.669
data for use because it takes a lot of time.

00:30:47.669 --> 00:30:49.229
So they want to avoid that.

00:30:49.229 --> 00:30:55.960
If they already know the scientist who collected
the data, they can skip this process.

00:30:55.960 --> 00:30:57.320
OK.

00:30:57.320 --> 00:31:06.769
My last point here is that, probably because
of this lack of trust in unpublished data

00:31:06.769 --> 00:31:14.369
and concerns about data quality, some participants
are accessing and downloading the data from

00:31:14.369 --> 00:31:20.059
the consortium database to the goal of comparing
these data to the ones they collected themselves.

00:31:20.059 --> 00:31:23.960
So the scientists are still collecting data
sets.

00:31:23.960 --> 00:31:30.019
And then they are downloading also, kind of
the same type of data set from the consortium

00:31:30.019 --> 00:31:35.700
database in order to make comparisons and
do some sort of quality control.

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:39.649
But they're not really reusing others' data
for knowledge production.

00:31:39.649 --> 00:31:44.330
So we can see that in this case, the application
of that for it is not avoided.

00:31:44.330 --> 00:31:50.109
It's a little bit twisted, so if you have
questions about this, in the Q&amp;A I can explain

00:31:50.109 --> 00:31:52.409
better.

00:31:52.409 --> 00:31:54.389
I don't want to give too many details right
now.

00:31:54.389 --> 00:31:57.429
But this is that idea.

00:31:57.429 --> 00:31:58.429
OK.

00:31:58.429 --> 00:32:00.399
So I just went out.

00:32:00.399 --> 00:32:06.159
I selected two disciplinary-specific concerns
about data reuse and I think they're interesting.

00:32:06.159 --> 00:32:13.029
So first of all, belonging to a specific domain
or having a particular set of skills really

00:32:13.029 --> 00:32:18.759
influenced the way in which the data were
accessed and reused by the community.

00:32:18.759 --> 00:32:20.129
And this seems obvious.

00:32:20.129 --> 00:32:24.789
However, whenever you make available data,
you really have to keep in mind that you have

00:32:24.789 --> 00:32:27.009
different types of users out there.

00:32:27.009 --> 00:32:31.590
And this means that you have to share data
in different formats, a different level of

00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:38.480
processing, provide different type of data
analysis tools and so on.

00:32:38.480 --> 00:32:45.109
And my last point is related to some concerns
about reusing data and the [? presumation

00:32:45.109 --> 00:32:46.109
?] of results.

00:32:46.109 --> 00:32:48.580
So let me explain you this.

00:32:48.580 --> 00:32:52.580
Some of the scientists-- and not all of them,
but some of the scientists-- involved in this

00:32:52.580 --> 00:32:59.629
collaboration, they work on particularly rare
syndromes that are characterized by scarcity

00:32:59.629 --> 00:33:00.629
of data.

00:33:00.629 --> 00:33:04.070
So in other
words, there is really no data or very few

00:33:04.070 --> 00:33:08.469
specialized data available for these syndromes.

00:33:08.469 --> 00:33:13.429
So what some scientists are doing, they are
reusing data collected by colleagues and available

00:33:13.429 --> 00:33:18.460
on the consortium database for their experiments.

00:33:18.460 --> 00:33:23.429
However, this data is close enough to what
they're looking for, but it's not exactly

00:33:23.429 --> 00:33:24.889
what they're looking for.

00:33:24.889 --> 00:33:27.889
Some other scientists are really concerned
about this practice.

00:33:27.889 --> 00:33:32.219
Because they are worried that by reusing the
that it is close enough, but is not exactly

00:33:32.219 --> 00:33:36.320
what they need, this could lead to misleading
results.

00:33:36.320 --> 00:33:42.799
So again I can give more specific examples
about these in the Q&amp;A.

00:33:42.799 --> 00:33:51.740
So in general, what I think we should-- especially
when we design policies for data sharing,

00:33:51.740 --> 00:33:57.389
reuse, or infrastructures for data sharing
and reuse, I think we should keep in mind

00:33:57.389 --> 00:34:01.859
that data reuse is not a homogeneous practice
but a set of practices.

00:34:01.859 --> 00:34:07.840
We should think about data reuse really as
a process, rather than as a one-time action.

00:34:07.840 --> 00:34:12.980
And then also we should keep in mind that
data are validated at multiple times during

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:19.470
the research process, and validating others'
data for reuse takes a lot of time and effort.

00:34:19.470 --> 00:34:23.140
Thank you, Irene.

00:34:23.140 --> 00:34:29.070
As you can see, this is going to be a very
important contribution to the literature of

00:34:29.070 --> 00:34:33.310
biomedicine as well as the literature of information
studies.

00:34:33.310 --> 00:34:38.160
So I thought this was a good preview of it
for this audience.

00:34:38.160 --> 00:34:42.169
And we also have several publications already
covering some of these topics that you can

00:34:42.169 --> 00:34:43.430
find and are linked.

00:34:43.430 --> 00:34:47.270
So let me give you just a few wrap-up slides.

00:34:47.270 --> 00:34:51.050
First, to summarize our research themes.

00:34:51.050 --> 00:34:55.500
The domains consist of multiple [? subdomains.

00:34:55.500 --> 00:35:01.220
?] And as you can see, again in this case
study, we've got many different areas within

00:35:01.220 --> 00:35:02.860
this biomedicine.

00:35:02.860 --> 00:35:12.040
And the fact that you've got four different
model organisms, which is a far more complex

00:35:12.040 --> 00:35:16.050
set of comparisons than is usually done in
biomedicine.

00:35:16.050 --> 00:35:21.790
It's a good comparison to areas like the earth
sciences, where people self-identify with

00:35:21.790 --> 00:35:24.360
50 or 60 different sub-domains.

00:35:24.360 --> 00:35:28.550
And still they're trying to share data, bring
them together, and use them.

00:35:28.550 --> 00:35:30.350
So big data is a problem.

00:35:30.350 --> 00:35:31.780
We all know that.

00:35:31.780 --> 00:35:35.640
But it's not the only part of the problem
is the bigness.

00:35:35.640 --> 00:35:42.710
It may be how long it is, how diverse it is,
what kind of distribution of geography, of

00:35:42.710 --> 00:35:49.090
language, of laboratories, of specimens are
involved as far as scale factors.

00:35:49.090 --> 00:35:56.520
The centralized collections-- things like
the astronomy ones, become decentralized in

00:35:56.520 --> 00:35:57.520
analysis.

00:35:57.520 --> 00:36:04.290
You might look at something as big and relatively
homogeneous as the Sloan Digital Sky Survey.

00:36:04.290 --> 00:36:10.640
But you may take pieces of that, and then
combine them with pieces from the Hubble Space

00:36:10.640 --> 00:36:13.770
Telescope, from Chandra, from Keck and others.

00:36:13.770 --> 00:36:17.530
So they become de-centralized when you start
to analyze them.

00:36:17.530 --> 00:36:23.370
The de-centralized data collections processes,
like this biomedical sharing, when you're

00:36:23.370 --> 00:36:28.250
trying to bring them back together and again
later, those are often the most difficult

00:36:28.250 --> 00:36:29.830
to integrate.

00:36:29.830 --> 00:36:36.000
And in that sense, some of the data reuse
problems in biomedicine are probably as difficult

00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:39.520
as almost any other science.

00:36:39.520 --> 00:36:45.630
And certainly of the ones that we've
looked at in the last decade or two.

00:36:45.630 --> 00:36:48.270
Some of the conclusions that we find.

00:36:48.270 --> 00:36:52.150
Data can be shared in many ways.

00:36:52.150 --> 00:37:00.150
Sharing point-to-point, sharing with your
colleagues, you have the best chance of transferring

00:37:00.150 --> 00:37:03.430
the knowledge-- particularly the tacit knowledge--
that goes with it.

00:37:03.430 --> 00:37:06.060
But that doesn't scale well.

00:37:06.060 --> 00:37:12.160
Something like this biomedical sharing hub,
where you're trying to scale by getting different

00:37:12.160 --> 00:37:18.020
groups around the country to put things into
[? commonhood, ?] scales better but you can

00:37:18.020 --> 00:37:21.480
see the kinds of trust issues that arise.

00:37:21.480 --> 00:37:26.500
We feel strongly that data sharing should
not be viewed as an end in itself, but rather

00:37:26.500 --> 00:37:28.780
a means to reuse.

00:37:28.780 --> 00:37:36.130
And much more research and public policy should
look at these questions around reuse.

00:37:36.130 --> 00:37:39.880
We know that reuse requires considerable knowledge
about the data.

00:37:39.880 --> 00:37:44.720
Not just what was collected, but why it was
collected.

00:37:44.720 --> 00:37:50.030
What the research questions were will tell
you quite a bit about what kinds of decisions

00:37:50.030 --> 00:37:51.340
that were made.

00:37:51.340 --> 00:37:58.270
The validation, the trust in the data, occurs
at multiple stages.

00:37:58.270 --> 00:38:01.860
Questions such as data quality, again, are
rising to the top.

00:38:01.860 --> 00:38:09.070
That data quality is at least as much a matter
of social trust and social relationships as

00:38:09.070 --> 00:38:14.480
it is anything that is inherent in the data,
per se.

00:38:14.480 --> 00:38:21.300
Stewardship and sustainability is something
we have not talked very much about today.

00:38:21.300 --> 00:38:27.850
And there is experimentation going on by NIH,
by NSF, by the European Commission, and others

00:38:27.850 --> 00:38:34.610
funding in this area about, should the responsibility
for stewardship live with the investigator?

00:38:34.610 --> 00:38:36.350
With the institution?

00:38:36.350 --> 00:38:38.100
With the collaborator?

00:38:38.100 --> 00:38:42.720
With the individually universities?

00:38:42.720 --> 00:38:44.800
With the funding agencies?

00:38:44.800 --> 00:38:47.030
And all of these different models are being
tried.

00:38:47.030 --> 00:38:54.630
The trust is definitely the overarching concern
when you think about data reuse.

00:38:54.630 --> 00:38:56.330
What does this mean for practice?

00:38:56.330 --> 00:39:00.390
Many if not most of you are trying to share
and reuse data.

00:39:00.390 --> 00:39:02.490
You may be running data repositories.

00:39:02.490 --> 00:39:08.850
You may be data scientists, data librarians,
archivists, research facilitators.

00:39:08.850 --> 00:39:17.500
Some suggestions here are to think not about
how is it done in astronomy, but how is it

00:39:17.500 --> 00:39:23.220
done in this particular kind of sub-domain,
and how does this sub-domain interact with

00:39:23.220 --> 00:39:24.980
others?

00:39:24.980 --> 00:39:30.330
We need to think about what the level of abstraction
is for data sharing and integration before

00:39:30.330 --> 00:39:33.970
we can think much about curation and reuse.

00:39:33.970 --> 00:39:36.220
Should we be thinking about point-to-point?

00:39:36.220 --> 00:39:43.810
Should we be thinking about contributing to
some large curated long-term memory model

00:39:43.810 --> 00:39:48.180
where you've really got a long-term commitment,
like libraries and archives?

00:39:48.180 --> 00:39:55.980
The sooner in a project that a group invests
in data curation, the more likely you are

00:39:55.980 --> 00:40:01.700
to create data that are going to be useful
indefinitely.

00:40:01.700 --> 00:40:08.450
Decisions made in the earliest stages around
what kind of documentation, what kind of data

00:40:08.450 --> 00:40:12.030
reduction you're going to do, will carry forward.

00:40:12.030 --> 00:40:18.270
If it's collapsed too soon, you will never
be able to separate those variables again,

00:40:18.270 --> 00:40:20.570
later for example.

00:40:20.570 --> 00:40:27.510
We encourage infrastructure solutions to think
about shared tools and services, and different

00:40:27.510 --> 00:40:31.050
kinds of discovery
mechanisms, recognizing that stewardship is

00:40:31.050 --> 00:40:32.850
going to have to be iterative.

00:40:32.850 --> 00:40:39.660
The way that you keep and manage data during
the course of a one-, two-, or five-year grant

00:40:39.660 --> 00:40:44.580
is going to be different than the way a lab
manages over the course of investigator's

00:40:44.580 --> 00:40:51.210
career, or how a university or a funding agency
manages long-term.

00:40:51.210 --> 00:40:59.740
Back to the kinds of questions being asked
internationally, the European Commission,

00:40:59.740 --> 00:41:04.490
through pretty much through a gauntlet at
the Research Data Alliance meeting in Barcelona

00:41:04.490 --> 00:41:11.590
two weeks ago, saying that the Ninth Framework
Project will invest somewhere on the order

00:41:11.590 --> 00:41:20.811
of 80 billion to 100 billion euros of public
money in research funding between 2021 and

00:41:20.811 --> 00:41:22.790
2028.

00:41:22.790 --> 00:41:29.740
All of that will be going into research that
is necessarily data-intensive and compute-intensive.

00:41:29.740 --> 00:41:36.690
They don't want to see every single group
trying to solve their data problems individually.

00:41:36.690 --> 00:41:43.190
They want to see much more in the way of community
efforts, of infrastructural solutions and

00:41:43.190 --> 00:41:44.190
approaches.

00:41:44.190 --> 00:41:50.290
And so the Research Data Alliance is but one
place where people are trying to come together

00:41:50.290 --> 00:41:56.820
to think about broader kinds of solutions,
rather than each lab, each group, each university

00:41:56.820 --> 00:41:59.690
trying to do these individually.

00:41:59.690 --> 00:42:03.970
Because these are very big and very large
problems.

00:42:03.970 --> 00:42:05.930
Let's end with just a slight of acknowledgements.

00:42:05.930 --> 00:42:07.950
Again, this is a team project.

00:42:07.950 --> 00:42:12.630
The six of us are the ones currently involved.

00:42:12.630 --> 00:42:17.730
Peter Darch, who is now an assistant professor
at the University of Illinois, Information

00:42:17.730 --> 00:42:24.150
School is leading the astronomy work and the
undersea biosphere work.

00:42:24.150 --> 00:42:31.820
Ashley Sands, who's now at the Institute for
Museum and Library Services as a program officer,

00:42:31.820 --> 00:42:35.580
has been working in the astronomy areas.

00:42:35.580 --> 00:42:38.510
Irene you've just heard from the biomedical.

00:42:38.510 --> 00:42:44.920
Bernadette, known as Bernie Randles, is working
in astronomy and computational sciences.

00:42:44.920 --> 00:42:51.840
And Milena Golshan, here in Los Angeles is
our data scientist and project manager who

00:42:51.840 --> 00:42:53.280
keeps all these pieces together.

00:42:53.280 --> 00:42:54.920
So, very much a collaborative effort.

00:42:54.920 --> 00:42:59.780
And we have had a number of other people graduate
from this group that we continue to collaborate

00:42:59.780 --> 00:43:01.010
with.

00:43:01.010 --> 00:43:02.740
So it's been a team effort.

00:43:02.740 --> 00:43:09.010
And I hope you will explore some of our publications
and other work that we have done that explores

00:43:09.010 --> 00:43:12.870
these questions across a number of different
disciplines.

00:43:12.870 --> 00:43:15.930
Let me stop there and leave 15 minutes for
questions.

00:43:15.930 --> 00:43:18.610
Because surely we have provoked a number of
questions.

00:43:18.610 --> 00:43:21.320
You have indeed, Christine.

00:43:21.320 --> 00:43:26.800
Thank you so much to Christine and Irene for
a really great survey of a lot of the data

00:43:26.800 --> 00:43:32.070
sharing activities which are going on, not
only in biomedicine but also in many other

00:43:32.070 --> 00:43:36.100
domains, too, struggle with the same problem
of this as you pointed out.

00:43:36.100 --> 00:43:41.260
This very rapid fire hose of data which is
coming at us and how we deal with that.

00:43:41.260 --> 00:43:46.390
How we share it, turn it outwards so that
it can be maximally used by people.

00:43:46.390 --> 00:43:48.340
A couple of questions have come in.

00:43:48.340 --> 00:43:55.390
And they are asking about the role of various
organizations and institutions on how to promote

00:43:55.390 --> 00:43:56.860
and encourage this.

00:43:56.860 --> 00:44:00.080
One particular-- I want to just extend that
a little bit.

00:44:00.080 --> 00:44:02.950
Because I don't
remember if you touched on this or not, but

00:44:02.950 --> 00:44:09.090
what's the role of journals in promoting data
sharing, encouraging it, and using shared

00:44:09.090 --> 00:44:13.040
data to support the content of the peer-reviewed
literature?

00:44:13.040 --> 00:44:17.260
Do you have any comments on that?

00:44:17.260 --> 00:44:22.960
Yes I do, and again we covered a lot of territory
here in a short time.

00:44:22.960 --> 00:44:29.360
Journals are certainly a very important part
of the infrastructure.

00:44:29.360 --> 00:44:36.120
And many journals, I think, pioneering in
biomedicine have required people to deposit

00:44:36.120 --> 00:44:42.810
data sets in public repositories as a condition
of funding the work.

00:44:42.810 --> 00:44:46.740
And that certainly promotes open science.

00:44:46.740 --> 00:44:55.890
The trade-off there is that we would like
to see those data be deposited in repositories

00:44:55.890 --> 00:44:57.650
that are open.

00:44:57.650 --> 00:45:03.330
So that you can get them and compare them
and look at them over time.

00:45:03.330 --> 00:45:09.800
If you publish things with a journal and the
data set only exists in, say, supplemental

00:45:09.800 --> 00:45:16.630
materials and it's behind a pay wall, it may
be useful for validating or verifying that

00:45:16.630 --> 00:45:18.280
journal article.

00:45:18.280 --> 00:45:23.680
But it may not be in some common standard
data structure where it can be reused by the

00:45:23.680 --> 00:45:25.230
community.

00:45:25.230 --> 00:45:33.690
So in our research, we would say that the
optimal model would be for the journal to

00:45:33.690 --> 00:45:40.360
require the data deposit, but to deposit in
a public repository, such as those run by

00:45:40.360 --> 00:45:45.700
the National Institutes of Health, and then
provide a link and relationship.

00:45:45.700 --> 00:45:55.080
Phil Bourne-- I think about 2005-- as the
editor of the PLOS Computational Biology journal,

00:45:55.080 --> 00:46:02.960
wrote a very nice little editorial talking
about how we should think about the database

00:46:02.960 --> 00:46:09.830
of journal literature and the database of
observational data as almost interchangeable.

00:46:09.830 --> 00:46:15.980
And the richer you could have those links
and relationships, the better you could [? mine.

00:46:15.980 --> 00:46:17.660
?] Start with the data, go to the journal.

00:46:17.660 --> 00:46:19.730
Start the journal, go to the data, and so
on.

00:46:19.730 --> 00:46:24.290
So let's think in terms of as synergistic
a relationship as possible.

00:46:24.290 --> 00:46:25.850
Great.

00:46:25.850 --> 00:46:31.700
One other question is-- some people are asking,
what sort of analyses are you seeing come

00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:34.260
out of the use of shared data?

00:46:34.260 --> 00:46:38.630
Then, how do you measure the impactfulness
of that reuse?

00:46:38.630 --> 00:46:43.120
Does that make sense?

00:46:43.120 --> 00:46:45.620
The impactfulness of re-uses.

00:46:45.620 --> 00:46:46.620
Yeah.

00:46:46.620 --> 00:46:52.750
So for example, can one measure the impactfulness
of, say, somebody taking data from a shared

00:46:52.750 --> 00:46:59.670
repository and, through their analysis they're
seeing a new publication coming out in, maybe

00:46:59.670 --> 00:47:03.070
Science or Nature-- a high-impact factor journal.

00:47:03.070 --> 00:47:07.430
And perhaps that's a measure of its potential
impactfulness.

00:47:07.430 --> 00:47:11.560
Or perhaps its being cited a lot so its citation
index is really big.

00:47:11.560 --> 00:47:14.310
I don't know, are you able to track that sort
of thing?

00:47:14.310 --> 00:47:18.300
Because it always seems that people are a
little bit wary about sharing data because

00:47:18.300 --> 00:47:20.250
they're not really sure it adds any value.

00:47:20.250 --> 00:47:24.320
Because once people have done their initial
analysis, that they've wrung all the useful

00:47:24.320 --> 00:47:25.320
stuff out of it.

00:47:25.320 --> 00:47:30.870
Are you getting a sense that there's value
beyond that initial publication done by the

00:47:30.870 --> 00:47:34.470
original authors that has some impact on the
field?

00:47:34.470 --> 00:47:38.450
Yes, so this is Irena.

00:47:38.450 --> 00:47:43.450
So the situation that you described is definitely
the ideal situation, where you have a scientist

00:47:43.450 --> 00:47:51.550
who is downloading a data set and running
a new analysis, finding new things, and publishing

00:47:51.550 --> 00:47:54.420
new articles with these new analyses.

00:47:54.420 --> 00:48:01.910
However-- so I've been observing and conducting
interviews with this community for almost

00:48:01.910 --> 00:48:03.610
three years now.

00:48:03.610 --> 00:48:08.030
The fact is that even data use takes a lot
of time.

00:48:08.030 --> 00:48:20.240
So at least in my case study, they just started
to reuse each other data, the scientists.

00:48:20.240 --> 00:48:28.750
But-- so first of all, it doesn't really happen--
or at least I haven't seen this so far-- that

00:48:28.750 --> 00:48:34.190
you have a scientist who goes on this repository
and downloads a data set and run a new analysis.

00:48:34.190 --> 00:48:36.870
It's always in the context of a collaboration.

00:48:36.870 --> 00:48:43.770
So first, what I'm really finding is that
scientists, they establish a collaboration.

00:48:43.770 --> 00:48:54.070
And then together, they work out the order
[? shape, ?] and who is taking care of which

00:48:54.070 --> 00:48:55.930
part of the new analysis.

00:48:55.930 --> 00:48:59.900
So even when you have a reanalysis, I think
it's always in the context of a collaboration.

00:48:59.900 --> 00:49:02.260
It takes a lot of time and effort.

00:49:02.260 --> 00:49:06.910
Yes, I think Chris wants to add something
here.

00:49:06.910 --> 00:49:13.800
I'm going to take that up to a higher level
and think about the different kinds of uses

00:49:13.800 --> 00:49:16.880
and re-uses.

00:49:16.880 --> 00:49:24.750
Looking at the impact or the contribution
of an individual reuse is no easier than looking

00:49:24.750 --> 00:49:27.920
at the contribution of any other kind of paper.

00:49:27.920 --> 00:49:31.240
So I think the measurement is always going
to be a challenge.

00:49:31.240 --> 00:49:33.950
We can look for those.

00:49:33.950 --> 00:49:41.130
One of the larger questions is the difficulty
of distinguishing between a use and a reuse.

00:49:41.130 --> 00:49:46.750
And we have a paper that just came out in
The Data Science Journal on exactly that problem.

00:49:46.750 --> 00:49:54.330
So if you think about people [? doing-- ?] building
data sets, some groups, their primary work,

00:49:54.330 --> 00:50:00.900
even in biomedicine, is to produce data that
other people can use.

00:50:00.900 --> 00:50:05.370
And so if those people weren't just plain
data producers, those data would not be there

00:50:05.370 --> 00:50:06.870
in the first place.

00:50:06.870 --> 00:50:14.020
Similarly, if some astronomers were not willing
to dedicate their careers to astronomy sky

00:50:14.020 --> 00:50:18.280
surveys, those data would not be there for
anyone else to use.

00:50:18.280 --> 00:50:25.000
So is the first use of evidence from a sky
survey or evidence from a genome survey, is

00:50:25.000 --> 00:50:27.020
that a use or is that a reuse?

00:50:27.020 --> 00:50:28.230
That is an unanswered question.

00:50:28.230 --> 00:50:33.560
So there is a lot of definitional issues here
before we start to try to put numbers on things.

00:50:33.560 --> 00:50:38.060
The other piece is on citation indicators.

00:50:38.060 --> 00:50:42.520
And data citation is something, again, we
put a lot of time into.

00:50:42.520 --> 00:50:49.330
I encourage you to look at Chapter 9 of my
book, which is on credit and attribution of

00:50:49.330 --> 00:50:53.760
data, which are really very different kinds
of things.

00:50:53.760 --> 00:50:58.800
People use data from other sources that they
do not cite.

00:50:58.800 --> 00:51:06.600
So for example, Irene's comparison between
pulling data to validate your own versus pulling

00:51:06.600 --> 00:51:09.080
data for a new research question.

00:51:09.080 --> 00:51:12.050
We found the same thing in [? SENS ?] 10 years
ago.

00:51:12.050 --> 00:51:19.050
Where, if they were using it for background
calibration, they were using it but they didn't

00:51:19.050 --> 00:51:20.170
cite it.

00:51:20.170 --> 00:51:23.160
It was part of a new research question, then
they would cite it.

00:51:23.160 --> 00:51:28.680
So whatever indicators you're seeing are a
floor.

00:51:28.680 --> 00:51:31.150
By no means are they a ceiling.

00:51:31.150 --> 00:51:35.560
And we're only at the beginning of people
actually putting things like digital object

00:51:35.560 --> 00:51:38.680
identifiers onto their data sets.

00:51:38.680 --> 00:51:43.530
Look at DataSite and look at the work the
California Digital Library and others are

00:51:43.530 --> 00:51:45.760
doing on indicators.

00:51:45.760 --> 00:51:51.380
So until people start being more consistent
about citing their own data and telling people

00:51:51.380 --> 00:51:57.280
where they are, and citing data they use from
other people, it's going to be difficult to

00:51:57.280 --> 00:51:59.040
get those kinds of indicators.

00:51:59.040 --> 00:52:06.310
That's great, we've had a couple of questions
with regard to data provenance as well as

00:52:06.310 --> 00:52:11.960
common data standards for storage and for
describing how data have been treated.

00:52:11.960 --> 00:52:19.030
This also links into the software tools and
other processing methods that people have

00:52:19.030 --> 00:52:21.480
used as part of their processing workflows.

00:52:21.480 --> 00:52:28.340
How important is it to link not only the data,
but to the tools and the software, utilities

00:52:28.340 --> 00:52:32.240
that people have performed on the data as
pertaining to tracking where that data came

00:52:32.240 --> 00:52:33.600
from and what's happened to it?

00:52:33.600 --> 00:52:40.930
Did Phillip Boren feed you 
that question?

00:52:40.930 --> 00:52:41.930
No.

00:52:41.930 --> 00:52:42.930
No.

00:52:42.930 --> 00:52:45.490
That's an absolutely essential question, and
again, something we could easily have delved

00:52:45.490 --> 00:52:48.930
into here and something that's of great concern
to us.

00:52:48.930 --> 00:52:56.240
And it's one Bernie Randles of our group,
her dissertation's going to look more at that.

00:52:56.240 --> 00:52:59.810
In many cases, if you don't have the software,
you don't have the data.

00:52:59.810 --> 00:53:01.660
I mean let's face it.

00:53:01.660 --> 00:53:07.370
It would be hard to find a research project
these days that doesn't have some kind of

00:53:07.370 --> 00:53:10.230
software associated with generating those
data.

00:53:10.230 --> 00:53:14.390
So you need to tie those together and you
need to link them.

00:53:14.390 --> 00:53:20.430
So things like the workflow models, Taverna
and others, the computational models, Carole

00:53:20.430 --> 00:53:25.460
Goble's work in Britain in biomedicine are
ones to look at there.

00:53:25.460 --> 00:53:29.950
We've particularly good looking at Jupyter
Notebooks and GitHub on some of the tools

00:53:29.950 --> 00:53:36.690
that people use to tie together their publications,
their data, and their software.

00:53:36.690 --> 00:53:43.560
And we have a piece that's in interview right
now on the Jupyter Notebooks in astronomy,

00:53:43.560 --> 00:53:47.060
and we'll certainly be looking at those in
biomedicine and others.

00:53:47.060 --> 00:53:49.090
Do you want to add that, Irene?

00:53:49.090 --> 00:53:52.780
Yeah, no I agree with everything you said,
Chris.

00:53:52.780 --> 00:53:59.320
We did these analyses of how people are using
the Jupyter Notebooks again, to cite their

00:53:59.320 --> 00:54:02.300
data and then to link the data with software.

00:54:02.300 --> 00:54:10.110
And again, what we are finding in this other
research is that even the ways in which you

00:54:10.110 --> 00:54:14.670
can use the Jupyter Notebooks and you can
store your data in different places and link

00:54:14.670 --> 00:54:19.430
it to the publication, [? through ?] the software,
it's not really standardized yet.

00:54:19.430 --> 00:54:24.230
There are many different ways in which you
can actually do that, provide both the data

00:54:24.230 --> 00:54:27.210
and the software as a package.

00:54:27.210 --> 00:54:33.880
And we are definitely looking into new tools
that can, and how efficient these tools are

00:54:33.880 --> 00:54:36.990
in enabling this process.

00:54:36.990 --> 00:54:37.990
Right.

00:54:37.990 --> 00:54:38.990
Certainly.

00:54:38.990 --> 00:54:40.310
That needs to be done.

00:54:40.310 --> 00:54:46.410
But let me put one note of caution there,
is the rush to standards can be

00:54:46.410 --> 00:54:47.410
risky.

00:54:47.410 --> 00:54:54.990
There's always going to be a trade-off in
the point at which you want to standardize

00:54:54.990 --> 00:55:01.510
so that you can really get that integration
and that interoperability in areas where you

00:55:01.510 --> 00:55:08.210
really still need to be pretty exploratory
and maybe not let 1,000 flowers bloom but

00:55:08.210 --> 00:55:11.280
let 500 bloom, where you're still working
it out.

00:55:11.280 --> 00:55:17.640
And if you standardize too quickly you're
going to lose certain lines of investigation.

00:55:17.640 --> 00:55:22.380
So this is one of the things we're seeing
in the undersea biosphere.

00:55:22.380 --> 00:55:27.650
It's pretty scarce data, emergent kinds of
standards and practices.

00:55:27.650 --> 00:55:32.130
It's a little too soon and they're wrestling
with getting standards there.

00:55:32.130 --> 00:55:37.400
Where astronomy is working from standards
called FITS files that were established in

00:55:37.400 --> 00:55:38.730
the late 1970s.

00:55:38.730 --> 00:55:45.200
And they're stretching those to the breaking
point and trying to decide what's the next

00:55:45.200 --> 00:55:48.119
step for them.

00:55:48.119 --> 00:55:50.510
I think those are very interesting points
about standards.

00:55:50.510 --> 00:55:56.050
And it always seems to me that-- I've been
involved with several standards groups that

00:55:56.050 --> 00:55:58.570
I've been participating with over the years.

00:55:58.570 --> 00:56:02.480
And just when you think you've got the standard,
you finally fleshed it out and whatnot, you

00:56:02.480 --> 00:56:06.030
say, OK ta-dah, here's the standard, there's
some other group that pops up like a mushroom

00:56:06.030 --> 00:56:08.690
after a rainstorm with, oh no we're going
to define the standard!

00:56:08.690 --> 00:56:11.970
And you end up getting a little bit of this
not-invented-here syndrome.

00:56:11.970 --> 00:56:17.860
Do you have any thoughts on how to socially
engineer that out or is it just to be expected?

00:56:17.860 --> 00:56:23.360
Well the good thing about standards is there's
so many to choose from.

00:56:23.360 --> 00:56:25.910
That's right Maybe that's just the way it
is.

00:56:25.910 --> 00:56:26.910
I don't know.

00:56:26.910 --> 00:56:30.340
But it would be nice be a little more uniformity
of it.

00:56:30.340 --> 00:56:33.910
I think what we need to do is bring the communities
together.

00:56:33.910 --> 00:56:39.980
And that's what's been so good about the Research
Data Alliance which has grown.

00:56:39.980 --> 00:56:46.119
This was established four years ago, but the
first meeting got about 300 people.

00:56:46.119 --> 00:56:54.550
This meeting in Barcelona two weeks ago, had
620 people from 45 countries.

00:56:54.550 --> 00:57:01.000
And the membership is up to 5,400 people from
almost 125 countries in four years.

00:57:01.000 --> 00:57:03.390
That's amazing.

00:57:03.390 --> 00:57:04.390
It is amazing.

00:57:04.390 --> 00:57:10.760
And it's the first place where people from
biomedicine and archeology and astronomy and

00:57:10.760 --> 00:57:18.090
agriculture and the United Nations could all
be in one room talking about questions like

00:57:18.090 --> 00:57:24.480
provenance and versioning and integration
and data citation and software side citation.

00:57:24.480 --> 00:57:28.760
And software sustainability and data sustainability.

00:57:28.760 --> 00:57:35.070
So I would encourage those of you interested
in questions of provenance and standards to

00:57:35.070 --> 00:57:40.010
get involved in RDA or other groups like that.

00:57:40.010 --> 00:57:44.220
There's national groups in addition to the
international.

00:57:44.220 --> 00:57:48.200
Before we reinvent the wheel too many more
times because we've done a lot of reinvention

00:57:48.200 --> 00:57:49.780
here.

00:57:49.780 --> 00:57:54.830
One final thing in our last couple of minutes
here is maybe each of you could comment on

00:57:54.830 --> 00:58:01.000
the role of data sharing,
of provenance, and the data science behind

00:58:01.000 --> 00:58:08.070
it all in education and training at our universities
and schools of higher learning, and the role

00:58:08.070 --> 00:58:11.170
that plays in training our next generation
of scientists.

00:58:11.170 --> 00:58:16.450
I think it's kind of an auspicious question
because we are on the eve of some of the marches

00:58:16.450 --> 00:58:18.840
for science which are going to be occurring
around the country.

00:58:18.840 --> 00:58:23.190
And I think that's probably an important message
to convey and I was curious if either of you

00:58:23.190 --> 00:58:25.400
had thoughts on that subject.

00:58:25.400 --> 00:58:26.400
Yes.

00:58:26.400 --> 00:58:33.730
So I'm currently helping Chris to teach this
class that she's been teaching for a long

00:58:33.730 --> 00:58:39.730
time which is Data Management And Creation
And Policies For Research Data.

00:58:39.730 --> 00:58:51.740
As a PhD student and future faculty in this
field, I really believe strongly in the role

00:58:51.740 --> 00:59:02.080
of education and educating researchers in
understanding all the practices and the policies

00:59:02.080 --> 00:59:07.030
around it to share and reuse, and how important
it is to make sure that their science that

00:59:07.030 --> 00:59:08.610
they are conducting is open.

00:59:08.610 --> 00:59:11.790
It's not just a matter of believing in open
science.

00:59:11.790 --> 00:59:13.650
But you really have to know also how to do
it.

00:59:13.650 --> 00:59:19.030
You really have to know how to curate the
data, what's submitted in the schema, what

00:59:19.030 --> 00:59:22.130
are the requirements from the funders, and
so on.

00:59:22.130 --> 00:59:25.780
So this is what Chris is teaching in her classes.

00:59:25.780 --> 00:59:33.090
And I think the students here at the Information
Studies School, they're very interested in

00:59:33.090 --> 00:59:34.690
this topic.

00:59:34.690 --> 00:59:43.810
We often talk about how this should be a mandatory
class for all undergrad or graduate students

00:59:43.810 --> 00:59:48.240
when they start approaching their research
context.

00:59:48.240 --> 00:59:53.330
Yes and you are definitely trying to involve
more and more students from around the campus

00:59:53.330 --> 00:59:55.330
and from different departments.

00:59:55.330 --> 00:59:57.270
Yeah, Chris.

00:59:57.270 --> 00:59:58.270
Perfect.

00:59:58.270 --> 01:00:03.530
Just to wrap that up-- that was a real gift
of a question, Jack.

01:00:03.530 --> 01:00:08.869
To wrap up on is I think it's a two-fold problem.

01:00:08.869 --> 01:00:15.450
One is, as around it has very articulately
said, we need to embed notions of open science

01:00:15.450 --> 01:00:20.160
and data practice in the graduate education
in pretty much every field.

01:00:20.160 --> 01:00:22.600
It needs to be part of research methods from
the beginning.

01:00:22.600 --> 01:00:29.580
But we also need to educate a generation of
data professionals.

01:00:29.580 --> 01:00:36.330
And the information studies approach is to
think about this entire life cycle of data,

01:00:36.330 --> 01:00:39.860
from the origins all the way through the long
term stewardship.

01:00:39.860 --> 01:00:45.520
Whereas most of the data science programs
are much more about computation and analysis

01:00:45.520 --> 01:00:46.930
and findings.

01:00:46.930 --> 01:00:53.320
So I would like to see us produce a whole
generation that can be part of the research

01:00:53.320 --> 01:00:58.390
teams, part of the university libraries and
archives, and part of the laboratory facilities,

01:00:58.390 --> 01:01:03.070
to really think big picture and help build
that infrastructure.

01:01:03.070 --> 01:01:06.870
And we're in early days, so I think we're
laying a good foundation.

01:01:06.870 --> 01:01:13.900
Well with that, I want to thank Christine
Borgman and Irene Pasquetto for an absolutely

01:01:13.900 --> 01:01:18.119
delightful presentation on data sharing, reuse,
and why it's hard to do.

01:01:18.119 --> 01:01:21.060
And with some potential solutions in there
too.

01:01:21.060 --> 01:01:22.060
Thank you so much.

01:01:22.060 --> 01:01:23.770
That was just outstanding.

01:01:23.770 --> 01:01:27.580
Really excited to have had you guys visit
with us today.

01:01:27.580 --> 01:01:28.780
Thank you everyone.

01:01:28.780 --> 01:01:33.280
And we'll see you next week at our next Big
Data [? 2 ?] Knowledge: Guide To The

01:01:33.280 --> 01:01:35.000
Fundamentals Of Science.

01:01:35.000 --> 01:01:36.320
Thank you again everyone.

01:01:36.320 --> 01:01:37.320
Have a great weekend.

01:01:37.320 --> 01:01:38.320
Thank you.

01:01:38.320 --> 01:01:38.599
Thank you.

